WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:18.740 align:middle line:84%
[SQUEAKING]
[RUSTLING] [CLICKING]

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:29.020 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: OK, so let's continue
our discussion of normed spaces

00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:30.010 align:middle line:90%
and Banach spaces.

00:00:30.010 --> 00:00:33.940 align:middle line:84%
So last time, I
introduced the space

00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:38.500 align:middle line:84%
of bounded linear operators from
one normed space from another.

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:42.200 align:middle line:84%
And we saw that when the
target is a Banach space,

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:44.680 align:middle line:84%
then this space of
bounded linear operators

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:45.865 align:middle line:90%
is also a Banach space.

00:00:45.865 --> 00:00:49.420 align:middle line:90%


00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:55.290 align:middle line:84%
So now, we'll look at
another example of a way

00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:59.430 align:middle line:84%
to obtain a normed space
from a given normed space.

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:17.265 align:middle line:84%
So I'll be talking about
subspaces and quotients,

00:01:17.265 --> 00:01:17.765 align:middle line:90%
all right?

00:01:17.765 --> 00:01:20.350 align:middle line:90%


00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:25.720 align:middle line:84%
So let me just recall
briefly what a subspace is.

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:27.610 align:middle line:84%
You should remember this
from linear algebra.

00:01:27.610 --> 00:01:31.510 align:middle line:84%
V will always be
at least a vector

00:01:31.510 --> 00:01:36.380 align:middle line:90%
space, usually a normed space.

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:39.050 align:middle line:90%
So we say a subset--

00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:40.730 align:middle line:84%
and I've been using
this notation.

00:01:40.730 --> 00:01:41.750 align:middle line:90%
It doesn't mean strict--

00:01:41.750 --> 00:01:44.380 align:middle line:90%


00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:45.490 align:middle line:90%
a strict subset.

00:01:45.490 --> 00:01:49.510 align:middle line:84%
I'm just kind of used to using
this notation from 18.100A.

00:01:49.510 --> 00:01:51.910 align:middle line:84%
And the textbook
use this notation

00:01:51.910 --> 00:01:56.080 align:middle line:84%
for just being a subset, not
necessarily a strict subset.

00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:59.820 align:middle line:90%
So quick note about that.

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:14.760 align:middle line:84%
So a subset of V is a subspace
if for all elements, two

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:23.420 align:middle line:84%
elements in the field of
scalars and elements in W,

00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:30.280 align:middle line:84%
their linear combination is
also in W. So W is closed,

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:32.620 align:middle line:90%
undertaking linear combinations.

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:38.650 align:middle line:90%


00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:43.020 align:middle line:84%
And so it's quite
easy to prove--

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:53.940 align:middle line:84%
and I'll leave this to you--
that the subspace of a normed

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:54.870 align:middle line:90%
space--

00:02:54.870 --> 00:03:02.460 align:middle line:84%
of a Banach space,
sorry, is itself

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:06.480 align:middle line:84%
a Banach space, meaning
it's complete with respect

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:13.830 align:middle line:84%
to the norm it inherits
from V. It's a Banach space

00:03:13.830 --> 00:03:26.080 align:middle line:84%
if and only if it's a closed
subset of V with respect

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:29.395 align:middle line:84%
to this metric we have
induced by the norm.

00:03:29.395 --> 00:03:35.970 align:middle line:90%


00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:40.590 align:middle line:84%
I mean, you can let me talk
our way through it real quick.

00:03:40.590 --> 00:03:43.470 align:middle line:84%
Assuming W is a
Banach space, we want

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:47.400 align:middle line:84%
to show W is also a
closed subset of V. So

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:51.980 align:middle line:84%
that means you need to
show, for example, that--

00:03:51.980 --> 00:03:53.570 align:middle line:84%
one equivalent way
of showing this

00:03:53.570 --> 00:03:56.600 align:middle line:84%
is that every sequence
that converges--

00:03:56.600 --> 00:03:58.370 align:middle line:84%
every sequence of
elements from W

00:03:58.370 --> 00:04:02.330 align:middle line:84%
that converges converges
to an element of W.

00:04:02.330 --> 00:04:08.600 align:middle line:84%
Now, take a sequence in W that
converges to some element in V.

00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:11.540 align:middle line:84%
We now want to show that
element's in W. Then

00:04:11.540 --> 00:04:14.330 align:middle line:90%
that sequence converges in W--

00:04:14.330 --> 00:04:18.980 align:middle line:84%
or is Cauchy in W. And
since W is a Banach space,

00:04:18.980 --> 00:04:21.560 align:middle line:84%
there must be an element
in W which it converges to.

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:23.870 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, by
uniqueness of limits,

00:04:23.870 --> 00:04:31.430 align:middle line:84%
that limit of that original
sequence must lie in W.

00:04:31.430 --> 00:04:34.310 align:middle line:84%
And now, going the
opposite direction,

00:04:34.310 --> 00:04:38.810 align:middle line:84%
assuming the subspace
is closed, we

00:04:38.810 --> 00:04:43.050 align:middle line:84%
want to show that now
this subspace is a Banach

00:04:43.050 --> 00:04:44.870 align:middle line:90%
space, meaning it's complete.

00:04:44.870 --> 00:04:47.690 align:middle line:84%
Well, let's take a
Cauchy sequence in W.

00:04:47.690 --> 00:04:50.630 align:middle line:84%
This is also a Cauchy
sequence in V. Therefore,

00:04:50.630 --> 00:04:52.130 align:middle line:90%
it has a limit.

00:04:52.130 --> 00:04:55.220 align:middle line:84%
This limit has to be in
W, since W is closed.

00:04:55.220 --> 00:04:58.580 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, every Cauchy
sequence in W has a limit in W.

00:04:58.580 --> 00:05:01.050 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, W
is a Banach space.

00:05:01.050 --> 00:05:03.332 align:middle line:84%
OK, so I didn't
write out what the--

00:05:03.332 --> 00:05:05.540 align:middle line:84%
I didn't write out the
details, but we talked our way

00:05:05.540 --> 00:05:06.140 align:middle line:90%
through it.

00:05:06.140 --> 00:05:09.830 align:middle line:90%


00:05:09.830 --> 00:05:12.290 align:middle line:84%
Since we're taking a
trip down memory lane,

00:05:12.290 --> 00:05:14.270 align:middle line:84%
I just recalled
what a subspace is.

00:05:14.270 --> 00:05:19.310 align:middle line:84%
Now, given a subspace, we can
obtain another vector space

00:05:19.310 --> 00:05:24.230 align:middle line:84%
with the data of the
space and the subspace

00:05:24.230 --> 00:05:25.500 align:middle line:90%
called the quotient.

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:27.320 align:middle line:90%
So let me recall the quotient.

00:05:27.320 --> 00:05:31.790 align:middle line:90%


00:05:31.790 --> 00:05:38.750 align:middle line:84%
So this is about briefly
recalling what the quotient is.

00:05:38.750 --> 00:05:58.040 align:middle line:84%
So let's take a
subspace of V. We

00:05:58.040 --> 00:06:05.090 align:middle line:84%
define an equivalence
relation on V

00:06:05.090 --> 00:06:14.260 align:middle line:84%
by saying that v is
related to v-prime if

00:06:14.260 --> 00:06:20.670 align:middle line:84%
and only if v minus
v-prime is in W.

00:06:20.670 --> 00:06:24.815 align:middle line:84%
Now, what do I mean by
equivalence relation?

00:06:24.815 --> 00:06:29.050 align:middle line:90%


00:06:29.050 --> 00:06:33.040 align:middle line:84%
v is always related to v
because the difference of v

00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:35.410 align:middle line:84%
is equal to 0, which
is an element of W

00:06:35.410 --> 00:06:40.940 align:middle line:84%
because W is the subspace
and therefore a vector

00:06:40.940 --> 00:06:42.140 align:middle line:90%
space in its own right.

00:06:42.140 --> 00:06:46.820 align:middle line:90%


00:06:46.820 --> 00:06:49.810 align:middle line:84%
If v is related to
v-prime, then this

00:06:49.810 --> 00:06:54.640 align:middle line:84%
implies v-prime is related to
v, simply because we-- again,

00:06:54.640 --> 00:06:58.120 align:middle line:84%
if this is in W, then
it's minus is also in W.

00:06:58.120 --> 00:07:04.250 align:middle line:90%
And so this is reflexivity.

00:07:04.250 --> 00:07:07.670 align:middle line:84%
And then we also
have transitivity,

00:07:07.670 --> 00:07:12.170 align:middle line:84%
that if v is related to
v-prime and v is related--

00:07:12.170 --> 00:07:15.350 align:middle line:84%
and v-prime is related
to v-double prime,

00:07:15.350 --> 00:07:19.340 align:middle line:84%
then v is related
to v-double prime.

00:07:19.340 --> 00:07:22.110 align:middle line:90%


00:07:22.110 --> 00:07:24.960 align:middle line:84%
Just simply take v
minus v-double prime

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:26.910 align:middle line:90%
and add and subtract v-prime.

00:07:26.910 --> 00:07:29.880 align:middle line:84%
Then you obtain the sum
of two elements in W

00:07:29.880 --> 00:07:34.510 align:middle line:84%
which must be in W. So this
is an equivalence relation.

00:07:34.510 --> 00:07:47.590 align:middle line:84%
And so then we define
the equivalence class

00:07:47.590 --> 00:07:51.880 align:middle line:84%
of v. This is the set
of all v-prime in V

00:07:51.880 --> 00:08:00.590 align:middle line:84%
such that v-prime
is related to v.

00:08:00.590 --> 00:08:07.390 align:middle line:84%
And then we define
a new set, v-mod W,

00:08:07.390 --> 00:08:09.565 align:middle line:84%
to be the set of all
equivalence classes.

00:08:09.565 --> 00:08:20.280 align:middle line:90%


00:08:20.280 --> 00:08:23.490 align:middle line:84%
And instead of writing the
equivalence class of v,

00:08:23.490 --> 00:08:38.760 align:middle line:84%
we typically write
v plus W instead

00:08:38.760 --> 00:08:42.330 align:middle line:84%
of the equivalence
class of v. So you

00:08:42.330 --> 00:08:45.150 align:middle line:84%
think of the
equivalence class of v

00:08:45.150 --> 00:08:50.460 align:middle line:84%
as being v plus
all elements of W.

00:08:50.460 --> 00:08:59.670 align:middle line:84%
Or two elements are the same if
they differ by an element of W.

00:08:59.670 --> 00:09:02.690 align:middle line:90%
So this is a set.

00:09:02.690 --> 00:09:19.170 align:middle line:84%
But it becomes a vector
space with the addition

00:09:19.170 --> 00:09:21.300 align:middle line:84%
and scalar multiplication
operations defined

00:09:21.300 --> 00:09:24.165 align:middle line:84%
in a kind of
natural way with v1.

00:09:24.165 --> 00:09:35.840 align:middle line:90%


00:09:35.840 --> 00:10:01.320 align:middle line:84%
And scalar multiplication is
just the equivalence class

00:10:01.320 --> 00:10:07.780 align:middle line:84%
given by lambda times v. Now,
you have to take a second

00:10:07.780 --> 00:10:10.220 align:middle line:84%
and make sure that these
operations are well-defined,

00:10:10.220 --> 00:10:12.340 align:middle line:84%
which I'm sure you
did in linear algebra.

00:10:12.340 --> 00:10:13.990 align:middle line:90%
What do I mean by well-defined?

00:10:13.990 --> 00:10:16.370 align:middle line:84%
Well, these are two
equivalent classes.

00:10:16.370 --> 00:10:18.490 align:middle line:84%
So if I take two other
representatives--

00:10:18.490 --> 00:10:20.770 align:middle line:90%
say v1-prime and v2-prime--

00:10:20.770 --> 00:10:24.790 align:middle line:84%
do I get the same
equivalence class

00:10:24.790 --> 00:10:27.940 align:middle line:84%
as I did with the unprime,
meaning is v1-prime

00:10:27.940 --> 00:10:32.470 align:middle line:84%
plus v2-prime plus W the
same equivalence class as v1

00:10:32.470 --> 00:10:34.420 align:middle line:90%
plus v2 plus W?

00:10:34.420 --> 00:10:38.065 align:middle line:84%
And this is not too
hard to say-- or to see.

00:10:38.065 --> 00:10:48.330 align:middle line:90%


00:10:48.330 --> 00:10:52.245 align:middle line:84%
So v-mod W, which is typically
how you pronounce this v

00:10:52.245 --> 00:10:55.120 align:middle line:84%
slash W-- v-mod W
is a vector space.

00:10:55.120 --> 00:11:07.780 align:middle line:84%
And so note, we can identify
W, so just the set W,

00:11:07.780 --> 00:11:11.690 align:middle line:84%
with the equivalence
class 0 plus W,

00:11:11.690 --> 00:11:29.970 align:middle line:84%
which is also equal to
little w plus capital W.

00:11:29.970 --> 00:11:39.415 align:middle line:84%
OK, so when we first
started talking about norms,

00:11:39.415 --> 00:11:42.380 align:middle line:84%
I also introduced what
was called a seminorm.

00:11:42.380 --> 00:11:46.970 align:middle line:84%
Now, a seminorm,
recall, satisfied not

00:11:46.970 --> 00:11:48.830 align:middle line:84%
all three properties
a norm satisfied.

00:11:48.830 --> 00:11:53.150 align:middle line:84%
It satisfies homogeneity,
meaning how scholars pull out,

00:11:53.150 --> 00:11:56.020 align:middle line:84%
and also the
triangle inequality.

00:11:56.020 --> 00:11:58.980 align:middle line:84%
But it didn't satisfy
positive definiteness,

00:11:58.980 --> 00:12:05.906 align:middle line:84%
meaning the seminorm could be
0 for certain non-zero vectors,

00:12:05.906 --> 00:12:06.406 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:12:06.406 --> 00:12:11.800 align:middle line:90%


00:12:11.800 --> 00:12:12.790 align:middle line:90%
How could that arise?

00:12:12.790 --> 00:12:14.080 align:middle line:90%
How could a seminorm arise?

00:12:14.080 --> 00:12:17.980 align:middle line:84%
Well, think of
taking the maximum

00:12:17.980 --> 00:12:22.600 align:middle line:84%
of the derivative of a
function as being a norm

00:12:22.600 --> 00:12:24.880 align:middle line:90%
or as being a potential norm.

00:12:24.880 --> 00:12:32.330 align:middle line:84%
Then it satisfies homogeneity
and the triangle inequality.

00:12:32.330 --> 00:12:36.670 align:middle line:84%
But it is not a norm
because the derivative

00:12:36.670 --> 00:12:40.810 align:middle line:90%
of constant functions is 0.

00:12:40.810 --> 00:12:43.540 align:middle line:84%
But constant functions
aren't identically 0,

00:12:43.540 --> 00:12:46.210 align:middle line:84%
if you think of them
as elements of, say,

00:12:46.210 --> 00:12:49.180 align:middle line:84%
the space of continuously
differentiable functions.

00:12:49.180 --> 00:12:52.330 align:middle line:90%


00:12:52.330 --> 00:12:55.180 align:middle line:84%
But this next theorem
says that if you mod out

00:12:55.180 --> 00:13:00.850 align:middle line:84%
by the elements in a vector
space on which the seminorm is

00:13:00.850 --> 00:13:06.100 align:middle line:84%
0, then you get essentially a
normed space where the norm is

00:13:06.100 --> 00:13:07.850 align:middle line:90%
given by the seminorm.

00:13:07.850 --> 00:13:12.370 align:middle line:84%
So if you mod out by the
elements with 0 seminorm,

00:13:12.370 --> 00:13:15.160 align:middle line:90%
you get an actual norm space.

00:13:15.160 --> 00:13:16.530 align:middle line:90%
The statement is let--

00:13:16.530 --> 00:13:21.702 align:middle line:90%


00:13:21.702 --> 00:13:36.600 align:middle line:84%
let's take a seminorm
on vector space V.

00:13:36.600 --> 00:13:44.580 align:middle line:84%
Then we define E to be the
set of all v in capital V such

00:13:44.580 --> 00:13:52.565 align:middle line:84%
that this seminorm of
these vectors is 0.

00:13:52.565 --> 00:13:57.840 align:middle line:90%


00:13:57.840 --> 00:14:11.070 align:middle line:84%
This is a subspace of V. And if
I define the following function

00:14:11.070 --> 00:14:15.170 align:middle line:90%
on v-mod E--

00:14:15.170 --> 00:14:18.130 align:middle line:90%


00:14:18.130 --> 00:14:22.010 align:middle line:90%
so what is it?

00:14:22.010 --> 00:14:26.570 align:middle line:84%
This is simply the norm
of the representative v

00:14:26.570 --> 00:14:29.750 align:middle line:90%
of this equivalence class.

00:14:29.750 --> 00:14:34.100 align:middle line:90%


00:14:34.100 --> 00:14:36.920 align:middle line:84%
So if I define a
function like this,

00:14:36.920 --> 00:14:55.060 align:middle line:84%
so then this function
defines a norm

00:14:55.060 --> 00:15:00.930 align:middle line:84%
on the space v-mod E. So in
short, if I have a seminorm

00:15:00.930 --> 00:15:05.820 align:middle line:84%
and I mod out by all the
elements with seminorm

00:15:05.820 --> 00:15:10.410 align:middle line:84%
equal to 0, then I get an actual
normed space, where this norm

00:15:10.410 --> 00:15:12.900 align:middle line:90%
is essentially the seminorm.

00:15:12.900 --> 00:15:23.070 align:middle line:90%


00:15:23.070 --> 00:15:25.090 align:middle line:90%
OK, so let's prove this.

00:15:25.090 --> 00:15:28.060 align:middle line:90%


00:15:28.060 --> 00:15:30.040 align:middle line:90%
So why is this a subspace?

00:15:30.040 --> 00:15:49.300 align:middle line:84%
Well, if I take two elements
in this space of elements that

00:15:49.300 --> 00:16:03.720 align:middle line:84%
have 0 seminorm and
two scalars, and if I

00:16:03.720 --> 00:16:12.600 align:middle line:84%
take the seminorm of the
linear combination form,

00:16:12.600 --> 00:16:14.280 align:middle line:84%
I want to show
this is equal to 0.

00:16:14.280 --> 00:16:17.670 align:middle line:84%
And this follows essentially
by homogeneity and the triangle

00:16:17.670 --> 00:16:18.400 align:middle line:90%
inequality.

00:16:18.400 --> 00:16:21.630 align:middle line:90%
This is less than or equal to--

00:16:21.630 --> 00:16:23.142 align:middle line:84%
if I apply the
triangle inequality

00:16:23.142 --> 00:16:24.600 align:middle line:84%
and then pull the
scalars out, this

00:16:24.600 --> 00:16:26.775 align:middle line:90%
is less than or equal to plus--

00:16:26.775 --> 00:16:30.380 align:middle line:90%


00:16:30.380 --> 00:16:34.820 align:middle line:84%
and if these both have seminorm
equal to 0, this is equal to 0.

00:16:34.820 --> 00:16:38.160 align:middle line:84%
And now, remember, a seminorm
is always non-negative.

00:16:38.160 --> 00:16:42.890 align:middle line:84%
So if I show something is less
than or equal to 0, it's also--

00:16:42.890 --> 00:16:44.270 align:middle line:90%
then it must be equal to 0.

00:16:44.270 --> 00:16:58.240 align:middle line:90%


00:16:58.240 --> 00:17:02.690 align:middle line:84%
So first off, we
define this function.

00:17:02.690 --> 00:17:07.839 align:middle line:84%
So now, let's show that this
defines a norm on v-mod E.

00:17:07.839 --> 00:17:10.720 align:middle line:84%
We need to first show it's
well-defined because I'm

00:17:10.720 --> 00:17:15.490 align:middle line:84%
defining it in terms
of a representative

00:17:15.490 --> 00:17:17.424 align:middle line:84%
of this equivalence
class, all right?

00:17:17.424 --> 00:17:35.180 align:middle line:90%


00:17:35.180 --> 00:17:37.070 align:middle line:90%
And what does this mean? i.e.

00:17:37.070 --> 00:17:43.550 align:middle line:84%
If, v plus-- if I have two
equivalence classes with--

00:17:43.550 --> 00:17:46.280 align:middle line:84%
or if I have the same
equivalence class with two

00:17:46.280 --> 00:17:50.210 align:middle line:84%
different representatives,
v and v-prime, then--

00:17:50.210 --> 00:18:03.360 align:middle line:84%
and I should write E,
not W. Then the norm of v

00:18:03.360 --> 00:18:07.830 align:middle line:90%
equals the norm of v-prime.

00:18:07.830 --> 00:18:10.100 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, this
function is well-defined.

00:18:10.100 --> 00:18:14.110 align:middle line:90%
Now, how do we do that?

00:18:14.110 --> 00:18:16.110 align:middle line:84%
Again, it's going to be
essentially the triangle

00:18:16.110 --> 00:18:16.920 align:middle line:90%
inequality.

00:18:16.920 --> 00:18:22.040 align:middle line:90%


00:18:22.040 --> 00:18:31.440 align:middle line:84%
So suppose v plus e
equals v-prime plus em

00:18:31.440 --> 00:18:33.510 align:middle line:84%
meaning I have two
representatives

00:18:33.510 --> 00:18:34.950 align:middle line:90%
for the same equivalence class.

00:18:34.950 --> 00:18:38.090 align:middle line:90%


00:18:38.090 --> 00:18:40.840 align:middle line:84%
And that means there
exists a little e capital

00:18:40.840 --> 00:18:47.890 align:middle line:84%
E such that v is equal
to v-prime plus e.

00:18:47.890 --> 00:18:52.360 align:middle line:84%
Then if I take the
norm of v, this

00:18:52.360 --> 00:18:55.270 align:middle line:84%
is equal to norm
of v-prime plus e.

00:18:55.270 --> 00:18:57.580 align:middle line:84%
And by the triangle
inequality, this

00:18:57.580 --> 00:19:06.180 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal to norm
of v-prime plus seminorm of E.

00:19:06.180 --> 00:19:10.560 align:middle line:84%
And now, since E is coming
from this set of all vectors

00:19:10.560 --> 00:19:14.430 align:middle line:84%
with seminorm equal to
0, this is equal to 0.

00:19:14.430 --> 00:19:18.310 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, I get
the seminorm of v-prime.

00:19:18.310 --> 00:19:22.350 align:middle line:84%
So I've shown that the
seminorm of v is less than

00:19:22.350 --> 00:19:24.510 align:middle line:84%
or equal to the
seminorm of v-prime.

00:19:24.510 --> 00:19:25.980 align:middle line:90%
Now, there was nothing--

00:19:25.980 --> 00:19:28.920 align:middle line:84%
this argument is symmetric
in v and v-prime, all right?

00:19:28.920 --> 00:19:32.580 align:middle line:84%
If v is equal to v-prime plus e
for some element in capital E,

00:19:32.580 --> 00:19:33.640 align:middle line:90%
so is v-prime.

00:19:33.640 --> 00:19:41.460 align:middle line:90%
So I can also switch the--

00:19:41.460 --> 00:19:43.740 align:middle line:84%
I can also switch v
and v-prime, meaning

00:19:43.740 --> 00:19:48.870 align:middle line:84%
I've shown that v is less
than or equal to v-prime

00:19:48.870 --> 00:19:53.490 align:middle line:84%
and the norm of v-prime is
less than or equal to v.

00:19:53.490 --> 00:20:00.980 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, their
two norms agree.

00:20:00.980 --> 00:20:09.190 align:middle line:84%
So this function
here is well-defined.

00:20:09.190 --> 00:20:12.220 align:middle line:84%
And so I'm going
to leave it to you

00:20:12.220 --> 00:20:18.250 align:middle line:84%
now to check that this function
now, which is well-defined

00:20:18.250 --> 00:20:22.720 align:middle line:84%
on v-mod E, is, in fact--
does, in fact, satisfy all

00:20:22.720 --> 00:20:25.000 align:middle line:90%
the properties of a seminorm.

00:20:25.000 --> 00:20:27.230 align:middle line:90%
I mean, it's non-negative.

00:20:27.230 --> 00:20:37.880 align:middle line:84%
So from essentially the
homogeneity and triangle

00:20:37.880 --> 00:20:40.850 align:middle line:84%
inequality property
of the seminorm,

00:20:40.850 --> 00:20:44.570 align:middle line:84%
the triangle inequality
and homogeneity of this

00:20:44.570 --> 00:20:46.250 align:middle line:90%
function then follow.

00:20:46.250 --> 00:20:51.470 align:middle line:84%
And we've essentially equated
all elements with seminorm

00:20:51.470 --> 00:20:53.040 align:middle line:90%
equal to 0 to 0.

00:20:53.040 --> 00:20:55.290 align:middle line:84%
So this is why it's
also positive definite.

00:20:55.290 --> 00:20:57.472 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to leave
that to you, OK?

00:20:57.472 --> 00:21:07.110 align:middle line:90%


00:21:07.110 --> 00:21:10.560 align:middle line:90%
OK, now, there's one other way--

00:21:10.560 --> 00:21:14.520 align:middle line:84%
if we're given--
so in this process,

00:21:14.520 --> 00:21:17.910 align:middle line:84%
we started with a seminorm
on the space, identified

00:21:17.910 --> 00:21:22.740 align:middle line:84%
the subspace of all, if you
like, zero norm elements,

00:21:22.740 --> 00:21:28.440 align:middle line:90%
and obtained a new normed space.

00:21:28.440 --> 00:21:32.580 align:middle line:84%
But you could start off with a
normed space, a closed subspace

00:21:32.580 --> 00:21:40.290 align:middle line:84%
of that normed space, and obtain
a new normed space on v-mod W,

00:21:40.290 --> 00:21:48.300 align:middle line:84%
if w is a closed space,
in similar fashion, where

00:21:48.300 --> 00:21:50.040 align:middle line:90%
I define this norm this way.

00:21:50.040 --> 00:21:56.850 align:middle line:84%
And well, the norm on that space
won't be the same as this one.

00:21:56.850 --> 00:21:59.700 align:middle line:84%
But that'll be in
the exercises, OK?

00:21:59.700 --> 00:22:02.560 align:middle line:90%


00:22:02.560 --> 00:22:03.060 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:22:03.060 --> 00:22:09.600 align:middle line:90%


00:22:09.600 --> 00:22:19.650 align:middle line:84%
So that's about-- this
is kind of concluding

00:22:19.650 --> 00:22:21.840 align:middle line:84%
the elementary section
of functional analysis,

00:22:21.840 --> 00:22:27.060 align:middle line:84%
meaning the bare bones of
Banach spaces and normed spaces.

00:22:27.060 --> 00:22:31.180 align:middle line:84%
And now, with the rest of this
lecture and then next lecture,

00:22:31.180 --> 00:22:35.640 align:middle line:84%
we're going to get into
the fundamental results

00:22:35.640 --> 00:22:41.140 align:middle line:84%
of functional analysis
related to Banach spaces.

00:22:41.140 --> 00:22:44.750 align:middle line:84%
So these theorems that I'll
now be stating and proving

00:22:44.750 --> 00:22:48.410 align:middle line:84%
in the coming couple of lectures
have names attached to them.

00:22:48.410 --> 00:22:51.400 align:middle line:84%
So you should definitely know
what they are, what they state,

00:22:51.400 --> 00:22:54.470 align:middle line:90%
what they don't state.

00:22:54.470 --> 00:22:57.050 align:middle line:84%
But to prove them, I
first need a result

00:22:57.050 --> 00:23:04.005 align:middle line:84%
from metric spaces, which
I did teach last semester.

00:23:04.005 --> 00:23:05.380 align:middle line:84%
But we didn't
cover this theorem.

00:23:05.380 --> 00:23:09.340 align:middle line:84%
I can't remember if I covered
it when I taught 18.100B.

00:23:09.340 --> 00:23:10.390 align:middle line:90%
I don't think I did.

00:23:10.390 --> 00:23:16.960 align:middle line:90%


00:23:16.960 --> 00:23:23.500 align:middle line:84%
So this is about
when can you write--

00:23:23.500 --> 00:23:27.290 align:middle line:90%


00:23:27.290 --> 00:23:28.340 align:middle line:90%
or in what way--

00:23:28.340 --> 00:23:31.550 align:middle line:90%


00:23:31.550 --> 00:23:35.680 align:middle line:84%
OK, so let me just
state that them.

00:23:35.680 --> 00:23:37.700 align:middle line:84%
And then I'll
interpret it for you.

00:23:37.700 --> 00:23:39.565 align:middle line:90%
So this is Baire's theorem.

00:23:39.565 --> 00:23:44.480 align:middle line:90%


00:23:44.480 --> 00:23:48.200 align:middle line:84%
It also goes by the name of
the Baire category theorem,

00:23:48.200 --> 00:23:51.560 align:middle line:84%
although it has nothing
to do with category theory

00:23:51.560 --> 00:23:57.130 align:middle line:90%
as is, I guess, used today.

00:23:57.130 --> 00:23:59.860 align:middle line:84%
I don't know if category
theory gets used

00:23:59.860 --> 00:24:02.170 align:middle line:84%
or if it just is
created for itself.

00:24:02.170 --> 00:24:05.010 align:middle line:90%


00:24:05.010 --> 00:24:13.920 align:middle line:84%
But Baire's category theorem
is the only category theorem

00:24:13.920 --> 00:24:16.808 align:middle line:90%
that I know.

00:24:16.808 --> 00:24:17.725 align:middle line:90%
So what does it state?

00:24:17.725 --> 00:24:21.290 align:middle line:90%


00:24:21.290 --> 00:24:26.900 align:middle line:84%
If M-- so again, this is a
theorem about metric spaces, so

00:24:26.900 --> 00:24:29.450 align:middle line:90%
not necessarily normed spaces.

00:24:29.450 --> 00:24:45.410 align:middle line:84%
So if M is a
complete metric space

00:24:45.410 --> 00:25:06.460 align:middle line:84%
and Cn is a collection
of closed subsets of M

00:25:06.460 --> 00:25:08.950 align:middle line:90%
such that M is their union--

00:25:08.950 --> 00:25:12.770 align:middle line:90%


00:25:12.770 --> 00:25:13.820 align:middle line:90%
so this is a--

00:25:13.820 --> 00:25:16.950 align:middle line:90%


00:25:16.950 --> 00:25:17.940 align:middle line:90%
so I have an N here.

00:25:17.940 --> 00:25:24.290 align:middle line:84%
So I should specify this
is N as a natural number--

00:25:24.290 --> 00:25:27.250 align:middle line:90%


00:25:27.250 --> 00:25:51.280 align:middle line:84%
then at least one Cn
contains an open ball B x, r.

00:25:51.280 --> 00:25:56.650 align:middle line:84%
So recall this is a
set of all y in M such

00:25:56.650 --> 00:26:01.630 align:middle line:84%
that the distance from
x to y is less than r.

00:26:01.630 --> 00:26:06.620 align:middle line:84%
So at least one Cn
contains an open ball

00:26:06.620 --> 00:26:20.480 align:middle line:84%
could be stated more succinctly
as at least one Cn has

00:26:20.480 --> 00:26:21.455 align:middle line:90%
an interior point.

00:26:21.455 --> 00:26:28.450 align:middle line:90%


00:26:28.450 --> 00:26:30.970 align:middle line:84%
So sometimes in applications,
these Cn's are not

00:26:30.970 --> 00:26:31.930 align:middle line:90%
necessarily closed.

00:26:31.930 --> 00:26:35.170 align:middle line:84%
But if M is equal to
the union of these sets,

00:26:35.170 --> 00:26:38.320 align:middle line:84%
then it's also equal to the
union of their closures.

00:26:38.320 --> 00:26:41.380 align:middle line:84%
And so what this theorem
says is that if you can write

00:26:41.380 --> 00:26:44.470 align:middle line:84%
a metric space as
a union of sets,

00:26:44.470 --> 00:26:47.770 align:middle line:84%
then the closure of
one of those sets

00:26:47.770 --> 00:26:49.390 align:middle line:90%
has to contain an open ball.

00:26:49.390 --> 00:26:52.880 align:middle line:90%


00:26:52.880 --> 00:26:58.100 align:middle line:84%
Now, let's think a little
bit of what it means for--

00:26:58.100 --> 00:27:02.090 align:middle line:84%
or there's a specific
terminology for when a closed--

00:27:02.090 --> 00:27:05.000 align:middle line:84%
when the closure of a set
does not contain an open ball,

00:27:05.000 --> 00:27:07.760 align:middle line:90%
that means it's nowhere dense.

00:27:07.760 --> 00:27:10.400 align:middle line:84%
Or that's the phrase we
usually use for that,

00:27:10.400 --> 00:27:12.590 align:middle line:90%
is that it's nowhere dense.

00:27:12.590 --> 00:27:15.290 align:middle line:84%
So Baire's theorem
says that if you

00:27:15.290 --> 00:27:20.570 align:middle line:84%
have M equal to the union
of a collection of sets,

00:27:20.570 --> 00:27:26.170 align:middle line:84%
then the closure of one of those
sets has to be dense somewhere.

00:27:26.170 --> 00:27:29.380 align:middle line:84%
Or you can't write M, a
complete metric space,

00:27:29.380 --> 00:27:32.815 align:middle line:84%
as the union of
nowhere dense subsets.

00:27:32.815 --> 00:27:43.630 align:middle line:90%


00:27:43.630 --> 00:27:46.750 align:middle line:84%
And so this theorem is
quite simple to state.

00:27:46.750 --> 00:27:53.560 align:middle line:84%
It's quite very
useful and, I mean,

00:27:53.560 --> 00:27:55.250 align:middle line:90%
quite powerful in applications.

00:27:55.250 --> 00:27:56.800 align:middle line:84%
In fact, you can
use this theorem

00:27:56.800 --> 00:28:01.750 align:middle line:84%
to give an alternative proof
to something that hopefully you

00:28:01.750 --> 00:28:03.250 align:middle line:84%
saw in your analysis
class, which

00:28:03.250 --> 00:28:06.040 align:middle line:84%
is that there exists a
continuous function which

00:28:06.040 --> 00:28:09.247 align:middle line:90%
is nowhere differentiable.

00:28:09.247 --> 00:28:12.110 align:middle line:90%


00:28:12.110 --> 00:28:13.700 align:middle line:84%
Maybe I'll put that
in the exercises.

00:28:13.700 --> 00:28:17.690 align:middle line:84%
Maybe I'll just point
you to it somewhere.

00:28:17.690 --> 00:28:19.810 align:middle line:90%
We'll see.

00:28:19.810 --> 00:28:21.880 align:middle line:84%
All right, so the proof
is by contradiction.

00:28:21.880 --> 00:28:28.802 align:middle line:90%


00:28:28.802 --> 00:28:30.760 align:middle line:84%
I believe this is the
first contradiction proof

00:28:30.760 --> 00:28:33.140 align:middle line:90%
that we've done in the class.

00:28:33.140 --> 00:28:36.820 align:middle line:84%
So in 18.100A and B, I
mean, you start off--

00:28:36.820 --> 00:28:38.440 align:middle line:90%
everything is by contradiction.

00:28:38.440 --> 00:28:41.240 align:middle line:90%


00:28:41.240 --> 00:28:46.570 align:middle line:90%
So let's suppose not, i.e.

00:28:46.570 --> 00:28:50.680 align:middle line:84%
you can find there
is a collection

00:28:50.680 --> 00:28:55.732 align:middle line:84%
of closed subsets of M such
that M is equal to this union.

00:28:55.732 --> 00:28:57.190 align:middle line:84%
And what we're
going to do is we're

00:28:57.190 --> 00:28:59.830 align:middle line:84%
going to find a point
that's not in this union

00:28:59.830 --> 00:29:03.130 align:middle line:84%
and therefore not in M. And
that will be our contradiction.

00:29:03.130 --> 00:29:07.570 align:middle line:84%
We'll use the completeness
of M to obtain this point

00:29:07.570 --> 00:29:10.030 align:middle line:84%
because how we're going
to obtain this point

00:29:10.030 --> 00:29:13.090 align:middle line:84%
is as a limit of a
certain sequence.

00:29:13.090 --> 00:29:16.690 align:middle line:84%
And to be able to say
that this limit exists,

00:29:16.690 --> 00:29:20.620 align:middle line:84%
we're going to show
the sequence as Cauchy.

00:29:20.620 --> 00:29:22.570 align:middle line:84%
All right, so we're
going to build up

00:29:22.570 --> 00:29:25.920 align:middle line:90%
this sequence inductively.

00:29:25.920 --> 00:29:30.300 align:middle line:84%
And I will write this
inductive proof kind

00:29:30.300 --> 00:29:31.920 align:middle line:90%
of carefully this first time.

00:29:31.920 --> 00:29:33.960 align:middle line:84%
And then in the
future, I'm just going

00:29:33.960 --> 00:29:37.590 align:middle line:84%
to say, OK, choose p1 this
way, choose p2 this way.

00:29:37.590 --> 00:29:40.920 align:middle line:84%
Continuing in this manner, we
obtain a sequence of points,

00:29:40.920 --> 00:29:41.920 align:middle line:90%
blah, blah, blah.

00:29:41.920 --> 00:29:46.200 align:middle line:84%
But at this stage, I'll
write this inductive proof

00:29:46.200 --> 00:29:47.530 align:middle line:90%
kind of carefully.

00:29:47.530 --> 00:29:49.560 align:middle line:90%
OK, so suppose not.

00:29:49.560 --> 00:29:55.430 align:middle line:90%


00:29:55.430 --> 00:30:10.640 align:middle line:84%
Since M certainly contains an
open ball and C1 cannot contain

00:30:10.640 --> 00:30:15.930 align:middle line:84%
an open ball, this implies M
does not equal to the first

00:30:15.930 --> 00:30:16.500 align:middle line:90%
closed set.

00:30:16.500 --> 00:30:19.380 align:middle line:84%
Otherwise, the first closed
set would contain an open ball.

00:30:19.380 --> 00:30:23.860 align:middle line:90%


00:30:23.860 --> 00:30:29.660 align:middle line:84%
Thus, there exists an
element p1 in M take away C1.

00:30:29.660 --> 00:30:31.340 align:middle line:90%
Now, C1 is closed.

00:30:31.340 --> 00:30:36.420 align:middle line:90%


00:30:36.420 --> 00:30:38.670 align:middle line:84%
This implies that its
complement is open.

00:30:38.670 --> 00:30:43.910 align:middle line:90%


00:30:43.910 --> 00:30:51.940 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, I can find
a small epsilon 1 such

00:30:51.940 --> 00:30:59.710 align:middle line:84%
that the ball centered
at p1 of radius epsilon

00:30:59.710 --> 00:31:06.025 align:middle line:84%
1 intersect C1
equals the empty set.

00:31:06.025 --> 00:31:14.910 align:middle line:90%


00:31:14.910 --> 00:31:16.260 align:middle line:90%
Now, I'm going to pick C2.

00:31:16.260 --> 00:31:23.610 align:middle line:90%


00:31:23.610 --> 00:31:33.420 align:middle line:84%
Now the ball centered at p1
of radius epsilon 1 over 3,

00:31:33.420 --> 00:31:37.740 align:middle line:84%
this is not contained in
C2 because, again, this

00:31:37.740 --> 00:31:38.800 align:middle line:90%
is by contradiction.

00:31:38.800 --> 00:31:40.320 align:middle line:84%
So we're assuming
that M is written

00:31:40.320 --> 00:31:42.930 align:middle line:84%
as a union of closed sets
and none of these closed sets

00:31:42.930 --> 00:31:44.130 align:middle line:90%
contain an open ball.

00:31:44.130 --> 00:31:46.440 align:middle line:84%
So this open ball cannot
be contained in C2.

00:31:46.440 --> 00:31:51.640 align:middle line:90%


00:31:51.640 --> 00:31:57.830 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, there
exists some point p2

00:31:57.830 --> 00:32:05.150 align:middle line:84%
in this open ball centered at
p1 of radius epsilon 1 over 3

00:32:05.150 --> 00:32:12.890 align:middle line:90%
such that p2 is not in C2.

00:32:12.890 --> 00:32:23.960 align:middle line:84%
Now, again, C2-closed implies
that there exists an epsilon 2

00:32:23.960 --> 00:32:27.950 align:middle line:84%
less than epsilon over 3-- so
we can make it very small if we

00:32:27.950 --> 00:32:29.720 align:middle line:90%
wish--

00:32:29.720 --> 00:32:37.800 align:middle line:84%
such that the ball centered
at p2 of radius epsilon 2

00:32:37.800 --> 00:32:41.880 align:middle line:84%
intersect C2 equals
the empty set.

00:32:41.880 --> 00:32:44.420 align:middle line:90%


00:32:44.420 --> 00:32:47.680 align:middle line:90%
So now, I've picked p2--

00:32:47.680 --> 00:32:48.640 align:middle line:90%
p1 and p2.

00:32:48.640 --> 00:32:50.950 align:middle line:84%
Now, at this point,
I would usually

00:32:50.950 --> 00:32:54.670 align:middle line:84%
say, continuing in this manner,
we obtain a sequence of points.

00:32:54.670 --> 00:32:57.280 align:middle line:84%
But let me write out
the argument carefully.

00:32:57.280 --> 00:33:02.820 align:middle line:90%


00:33:02.820 --> 00:33:09.450 align:middle line:90%
Suppose there exists k points--

00:33:09.450 --> 00:33:31.210 align:middle line:84%
p1, pk and positive epsilon 1,
epsilon k such that two things

00:33:31.210 --> 00:33:32.120 align:middle line:90%
occur.

00:33:32.120 --> 00:33:41.150 align:middle line:84%
So epsilon k is less than
epsilon k minus 1 over 3 and--

00:33:41.150 --> 00:33:43.810 align:middle line:90%


00:33:43.810 --> 00:33:48.610 align:middle line:84%
which is less than epsilon
k minus 2 over 3 and so

00:33:48.610 --> 00:33:55.255 align:middle line:84%
on all the way down to
epsilon 1 over 3 k minus 1.

00:33:55.255 --> 00:33:58.650 align:middle line:90%


00:33:58.650 --> 00:34:01.800 align:middle line:84%
So if you like, k here is
bigger than or equal to 2.

00:34:01.800 --> 00:34:05.860 align:middle line:90%


00:34:05.860 --> 00:34:17.139 align:middle line:84%
And pj satisfies-- pj is in
the ball centered at pj minus 1

00:34:17.139 --> 00:34:23.010 align:middle line:84%
of radius epsilon
j minus 1 over 3.

00:34:23.010 --> 00:34:30.179 align:middle line:84%
And the ball centered
at pj epsilon j

00:34:30.179 --> 00:34:34.350 align:middle line:84%
intersects Cj equals
the empty set.

00:34:34.350 --> 00:34:37.389 align:middle line:90%


00:34:37.389 --> 00:34:45.120 align:middle line:84%
So let me star those two
properties right there.

00:34:45.120 --> 00:34:50.219 align:middle line:84%
We're going to obtain another
point satisfying those two

00:34:50.219 --> 00:34:52.020 align:middle line:90%
properties.

00:34:52.020 --> 00:34:59.720 align:middle line:84%
And I don't know why
I'm being a stickler.

00:34:59.720 --> 00:35:01.020 align:middle line:90%
It doesn't really matter.

00:35:01.020 --> 00:35:05.390 align:middle line:84%
But let me put a 2
because that's not

00:35:05.390 --> 00:35:07.160 align:middle line:90%
necessarily satisfied for p1.

00:35:07.160 --> 00:35:11.880 align:middle line:84%
But OK, now, I want
to show there exists--

00:35:11.880 --> 00:35:17.060 align:middle line:84%
I can obtain a k plus first
point satisfying those two

00:35:17.060 --> 00:35:19.010 align:middle line:90%
properties.

00:35:19.010 --> 00:35:20.780 align:middle line:84%
Again, this is just
formally saying

00:35:20.780 --> 00:35:24.320 align:middle line:84%
that once I've chosen
p2, then I can choose

00:35:24.320 --> 00:35:31.480 align:middle line:90%
a p3 doing this argument again.

00:35:31.480 --> 00:35:35.680 align:middle line:84%
And it'll satisfy everything
p2 satisfied with epsilon 1,

00:35:35.680 --> 00:35:37.750 align:middle line:90%
except now with epsilon 2.

00:35:37.750 --> 00:35:39.860 align:middle line:84%
But now, I'm just
making it more formal.

00:35:39.860 --> 00:35:46.460 align:middle line:84%
So now, again, M is the union
of all these closed sets, none

00:35:46.460 --> 00:35:49.960 align:middle line:90%
of which contain an open ball.

00:35:49.960 --> 00:35:59.830 align:middle line:84%
So since the ball of
radius pk epsilon k over 3

00:35:59.830 --> 00:36:06.890 align:middle line:84%
is not contained
in Ck plus 1, there

00:36:06.890 --> 00:36:15.900 align:middle line:84%
exists an element pk
plus 1 not in Ck plus 1.

00:36:15.900 --> 00:36:16.800 align:middle line:90%
Or I should say--

00:36:16.800 --> 00:36:27.756 align:middle line:90%


00:36:27.756 --> 00:36:42.280 align:middle line:84%
over 3-- such that pk plus
1 is not in Ck plus 1.

00:36:42.280 --> 00:36:59.950 align:middle line:84%
So let me-- there's pk
epsilon k over 3, Ck plus 1.

00:36:59.950 --> 00:37:01.955 align:middle line:90%
So maybe they're not disjoint.

00:37:01.955 --> 00:37:03.080 align:middle line:90%
Maybe there's some overlap.

00:37:03.080 --> 00:37:16.890 align:middle line:84%
But then I can find
a point pk plus 1

00:37:16.890 --> 00:37:19.050 align:middle line:84%
in there that's
not in Ck plus 1.

00:37:19.050 --> 00:37:24.780 align:middle line:90%


00:37:24.780 --> 00:37:31.250 align:middle line:84%
So then there exists
an epsilon k plus 1,

00:37:31.250 --> 00:37:33.410 align:middle line:84%
which I can choose
very small, say smaller

00:37:33.410 --> 00:37:38.400 align:middle line:84%
than epsilon k
over 3, such that--

00:37:38.400 --> 00:37:42.800 align:middle line:84%
again, because Ck plus
1 is closed, pk plus 1

00:37:42.800 --> 00:37:44.810 align:middle line:90%
is not in Ck plus 1.

00:37:44.810 --> 00:37:47.900 align:middle line:84%
So I can find a
small ball around pk

00:37:47.900 --> 00:37:52.028 align:middle line:90%
plus 1 disjoint from Ck plus 1--

00:37:52.028 --> 00:38:08.660 align:middle line:84%
a ball-- we said Ck
equals the empty set.

00:38:08.660 --> 00:38:15.170 align:middle line:90%


00:38:15.170 --> 00:38:18.740 align:middle line:84%
So then given k
points, I can then

00:38:18.740 --> 00:38:23.600 align:middle line:84%
choose a k plus first point
satisfying these two bullet

00:38:23.600 --> 00:38:25.610 align:middle line:90%
points here, all right?

00:38:25.610 --> 00:38:28.240 align:middle line:90%


00:38:28.240 --> 00:38:54.860 align:middle line:84%
So by induction, we have found
a sequence of points, pk,

00:38:54.860 --> 00:38:58.000 align:middle line:90%
in M such that--

00:38:58.000 --> 00:39:03.210 align:middle line:90%
and epsilon k.

00:39:03.210 --> 00:39:05.160 align:middle line:90%
So if you like, k starts at 2.

00:39:05.160 --> 00:39:08.060 align:middle line:90%


00:39:08.060 --> 00:39:19.230 align:middle line:84%
Epsilon k and 0 epsilon
1 such that for all k,

00:39:19.230 --> 00:39:21.780 align:middle line:84%
those two bullet points
hold, such that--

00:39:21.780 --> 00:39:26.380 align:middle line:90%


00:39:26.380 --> 00:39:28.470 align:middle line:84%
which I denoted-- which
I put a little star by,

00:39:28.470 --> 00:39:30.762 align:middle line:84%
star [? hold. ?] So I'm
talking about these two points.

00:39:30.762 --> 00:39:34.800 align:middle line:90%


00:39:34.800 --> 00:39:40.615 align:middle line:84%
So when I say star, I mean
these two statements here, OK?

00:39:40.615 --> 00:39:57.140 align:middle line:90%


00:39:57.140 --> 00:40:04.700 align:middle line:84%
OK, now, let's show that
the sequence is Cauchy.

00:40:04.700 --> 00:40:05.630 align:middle line:90%
So now, I claim--

00:40:05.630 --> 00:40:16.010 align:middle line:90%


00:40:16.010 --> 00:40:16.765 align:middle line:90%
how do we do that?

00:40:16.765 --> 00:40:21.540 align:middle line:90%


00:40:21.540 --> 00:40:24.500 align:middle line:90%
This follows.

00:40:24.500 --> 00:40:27.173 align:middle line:84%
So I'm not going to write out
the full epsilon M argument.

00:40:27.173 --> 00:40:29.590 align:middle line:84%
But I'm just going to write
down the crucial estimate that

00:40:29.590 --> 00:40:30.340 align:middle line:90%
proves this.

00:40:30.340 --> 00:40:40.380 align:middle line:84%
This follows from the
fact that for all k

00:40:40.380 --> 00:40:47.470 align:middle line:84%
for all l, If I look at the
distance between pk and pk

00:40:47.470 --> 00:40:50.290 align:middle line:84%
plus l because in the
end, when I look at--

00:40:50.290 --> 00:40:52.060 align:middle line:84%
if I want to show
something's Cauchy,

00:40:52.060 --> 00:40:53.950 align:middle line:84%
I need to take the
difference of two--

00:40:53.950 --> 00:40:57.470 align:middle line:84%
or the distance
between two points.

00:40:57.470 --> 00:41:00.530 align:middle line:84%
One I can choose as
the point occurring

00:41:00.530 --> 00:41:03.680 align:middle line:84%
earlier in the sequence, and
then one occurring later.

00:41:03.680 --> 00:41:07.700 align:middle line:84%
Anyways, then by the
triangle inequality repeated,

00:41:07.700 --> 00:41:10.580 align:middle line:84%
I can write that this is less
than or equal to the distance

00:41:10.580 --> 00:41:16.610 align:middle line:84%
from pk pk plus 1 plus
the distance from pk

00:41:16.610 --> 00:41:19.100 align:middle line:90%
plus 1 pk plus l.

00:41:19.100 --> 00:41:22.010 align:middle line:84%
And now, I could do the
triangle inequality again.

00:41:22.010 --> 00:41:27.710 align:middle line:84%
And so I get that this is less
than or equal to the distance

00:41:27.710 --> 00:41:33.350 align:middle line:84%
from pk to pk plus 1 plus
the distance from pk plus 1

00:41:33.350 --> 00:41:41.570 align:middle line:84%
to pk plus 2 and so on until I
get to the distance between pk

00:41:41.570 --> 00:41:45.612 align:middle line:90%
plus l minus 1 and pk plus l.

00:41:45.612 --> 00:41:50.960 align:middle line:90%


00:41:50.960 --> 00:41:56.930 align:middle line:84%
Now, by star, what's
in yellow, pk plus 1

00:41:56.930 --> 00:42:01.670 align:middle line:84%
is in the epsilon k over
3 ball centered at pk.

00:42:01.670 --> 00:42:06.410 align:middle line:84%
So this is less than
epsilon k over 3.

00:42:06.410 --> 00:42:12.110 align:middle line:84%
And the same thing here, less
than epsilon k plus 1 over 3

00:42:12.110 --> 00:42:20.350 align:middle line:84%
plus epsilon k plus
l minus 1 over 3.

00:42:20.350 --> 00:42:24.730 align:middle line:84%
And now, all of
those are less than--

00:42:24.730 --> 00:42:27.220 align:middle line:84%
if you go to the first one,
it's less than epsilon 1

00:42:27.220 --> 00:42:30.520 align:middle line:90%
over 3 k minus 1.

00:42:30.520 --> 00:42:37.910 align:middle line:84%
So then this is less
than epsilon 1 over 3 k

00:42:37.910 --> 00:42:46.790 align:middle line:84%
plus epsilon 1 over
3 k plus l, yeah.

00:42:46.790 --> 00:42:52.190 align:middle line:90%


00:42:52.190 --> 00:42:55.610 align:middle line:84%
And now, I can
actually sum this up.

00:42:55.610 --> 00:42:59.990 align:middle line:84%
This is strictly
less than epsilon 1.

00:42:59.990 --> 00:43:06.260 align:middle line:84%
And if I sum now from l equals
k to infinity of 1 over 3 k,

00:43:06.260 --> 00:43:16.950 align:middle line:84%
this is equal to 1 over epsilon
1 3 k 1 over 1 minus 1/3,

00:43:16.950 --> 00:43:27.680 align:middle line:84%
which equals epsilon 1 over
2 3 to the minus k plus 1.

00:43:27.680 --> 00:43:31.850 align:middle line:84%
So the distance between
a point pk and pk plus l

00:43:31.850 --> 00:43:38.210 align:middle line:84%
is less than some constant
times 3 to the minus k plus 1.

00:43:38.210 --> 00:43:44.000 align:middle line:84%
So if k is very large, this is
very small independent of l.

00:43:44.000 --> 00:43:45.870 align:middle line:90%
So I shouldn't have used l here.

00:43:45.870 --> 00:43:55.405 align:middle line:84%
And that's M, M. So this shows
that the sequence is Cauchy.

00:43:55.405 --> 00:44:05.690 align:middle line:90%


00:44:05.690 --> 00:44:14.430 align:middle line:84%
So since M is complete,
there exists a p in M such

00:44:14.430 --> 00:44:20.100 align:middle line:84%
that these elements
pk converge to p.

00:44:20.100 --> 00:44:24.120 align:middle line:84%
And now, we're going to show
this point p does not lie

00:44:24.120 --> 00:44:26.195 align:middle line:90%
in any of the CJ's.

00:44:26.195 --> 00:44:29.810 align:middle line:90%


00:44:29.810 --> 00:44:32.990 align:middle line:84%
And we'll do it by showing
it's essentially in all

00:44:32.990 --> 00:44:37.510 align:middle line:90%
of these balls, pj epsilon j.

00:44:37.510 --> 00:44:42.030 align:middle line:90%


00:44:42.030 --> 00:44:44.220 align:middle line:84%
All right, and it's kind
a similar computation

00:44:44.220 --> 00:44:45.610 align:middle line:90%
to what we just did.

00:44:45.610 --> 00:45:02.170 align:middle line:84%
So now, for all
k natural number,

00:45:02.170 --> 00:45:07.210 align:middle line:84%
if you look at the
distance between pk plus 1,

00:45:07.210 --> 00:45:09.130 align:middle line:90%
pk plus 1 plus l--

00:45:09.130 --> 00:45:12.390 align:middle line:90%


00:45:12.390 --> 00:45:20.210 align:middle line:84%
again, so if I just go back to
this computation right here,

00:45:20.210 --> 00:45:26.000 align:middle line:84%
this, we proved, was less
than epsilon k plus 1 times 1

00:45:26.000 --> 00:45:31.310 align:middle line:90%
over 3 k plus 1 plus--

00:45:31.310 --> 00:45:39.070 align:middle line:84%
or actually, 1 over
3 plus 1 over 3

00:45:39.070 --> 00:45:45.250 align:middle line:90%
squared plus 1 over 3 k plus 1--

00:45:45.250 --> 00:45:48.660 align:middle line:90%


00:45:48.660 --> 00:45:49.545 align:middle line:90%
k plus l.

00:45:49.545 --> 00:45:56.850 align:middle line:90%


00:45:56.850 --> 00:45:58.755 align:middle line:84%
Or I'm sorry, I
think that's just l.

00:45:58.755 --> 00:46:02.440 align:middle line:90%


00:46:02.440 --> 00:46:06.710 align:middle line:84%
And now, this thing is
less than if I replace it

00:46:06.710 --> 00:46:08.220 align:middle line:90%
by the infinite sum.

00:46:08.220 --> 00:46:13.970 align:middle line:84%
So this is less than epsilon k
plus 1 times the infinite sum

00:46:13.970 --> 00:46:18.380 align:middle line:84%
again, m equals 0 to
infinity 3 to the minus

00:46:18.380 --> 00:46:21.785 align:middle line:90%
m, which equals what?

00:46:21.785 --> 00:46:26.490 align:middle line:90%


00:46:26.490 --> 00:46:32.310 align:middle line:84%
So this is, again, equal to
epsilon k plus 1 times 3/2.

00:46:32.310 --> 00:46:57.220 align:middle line:90%


00:46:57.220 --> 00:47:02.120 align:middle line:84%
OK, so we've proven that the
distance between pk plus 1

00:47:02.120 --> 00:47:06.290 align:middle line:84%
pk plus 1 plus l is
less than epsilon

00:47:06.290 --> 00:47:11.600 align:middle line:90%
k plus 1 times 3 over 2.

00:47:11.600 --> 00:47:15.120 align:middle line:90%


00:47:15.120 --> 00:47:18.480 align:middle line:84%
Let me take the limit
as l goes to infinity.

00:47:18.480 --> 00:47:23.720 align:middle line:90%


00:47:23.720 --> 00:47:27.050 align:middle line:84%
pk plus 1 plus l
converges to the point p.

00:47:27.050 --> 00:47:33.710 align:middle line:84%
So I get that the distance
between pk plus 1 and the point

00:47:33.710 --> 00:47:42.540 align:middle line:84%
p is less than or equal
to 3/2 epsilon k plus 1.

00:47:42.540 --> 00:47:49.330 align:middle line:84%
And remember, epsilon k plus 1
is less than a third epsilon k.

00:47:49.330 --> 00:47:57.210 align:middle line:84%
So this is less than 1/2
epsilon k, all right?

00:47:57.210 --> 00:47:59.460 align:middle line:84%
And since pk plus
1-- remember, this

00:47:59.460 --> 00:48:04.800 align:middle line:84%
is in the ball of
radius 13 epsilon k.

00:48:04.800 --> 00:48:10.630 align:middle line:84%
So I get that the
distance between pk and p

00:48:10.630 --> 00:48:16.350 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal to
the distance between pk

00:48:16.350 --> 00:48:25.240 align:middle line:90%
and pk plus 1 plus 1 and p.

00:48:25.240 --> 00:48:28.510 align:middle line:84%
We get this is less than
or equal to 1/3 epsilon

00:48:28.510 --> 00:48:31.120 align:middle line:90%
k plus 1/2 epsilon k.

00:48:31.120 --> 00:48:34.510 align:middle line:84%
And this is less
than epsilon k, which

00:48:34.510 --> 00:48:46.070 align:middle line:84%
means that p is in the ball of
radius pk of radius epsilon k,

00:48:46.070 --> 00:48:52.020 align:middle line:84%
which by the second
property up in star--

00:48:52.020 --> 00:48:54.590 align:middle line:84%
the fact that all these
balls are disjoint from--

00:48:54.590 --> 00:48:59.750 align:middle line:84%
each of the balls is disjoint
from Ck means p is not in Ck.

00:48:59.750 --> 00:49:03.420 align:middle line:90%
Now, k was arbitrary here.

00:49:03.420 --> 00:49:12.320 align:middle line:84%
So we conclude that p is
not in this union, which

00:49:12.320 --> 00:49:15.890 align:middle line:84%
is equal to M. And
that's a contradiction.

00:49:15.890 --> 00:49:26.000 align:middle line:90%


00:49:26.000 --> 00:49:30.620 align:middle line:84%
So I mean, I said the
strategy at the top

00:49:30.620 --> 00:49:34.850 align:middle line:84%
of the proof, the technical
argument maybe muddled

00:49:34.850 --> 00:49:35.760 align:middle line:90%
what was going on.

00:49:35.760 --> 00:49:42.540 align:middle line:84%
But again, the point
is you can build--

00:49:42.540 --> 00:49:44.190 align:middle line:84%
if this conclusion
does not hold,

00:49:44.190 --> 00:49:49.890 align:middle line:84%
you can build a sequence which
has this disjointness property

00:49:49.890 --> 00:49:53.930 align:middle line:84%
from this collection
of open sets

00:49:53.930 --> 00:49:56.150 align:middle line:84%
such that the
sequence is Cauchy.

00:49:56.150 --> 00:49:58.520 align:middle line:84%
And because M is a
complete metric space,

00:49:58.520 --> 00:50:00.530 align:middle line:90%
you can then extract a limit p.

00:50:00.530 --> 00:50:04.160 align:middle line:84%
This limit p will have all the
properties that these pk's had,

00:50:04.160 --> 00:50:07.310 align:middle line:84%
namely that they're
not in any of the Ck's.

00:50:07.310 --> 00:50:10.490 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, this
point p does not

00:50:10.490 --> 00:50:13.670 align:middle line:84%
lie in the metric space,
which is nonsense.

00:50:13.670 --> 00:50:15.260 align:middle line:84%
So we arrived at
our contradiction.

00:50:15.260 --> 00:50:19.190 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, Baire's
category theorem is proven.

00:50:19.190 --> 00:50:28.320 align:middle line:90%


00:50:28.320 --> 00:50:35.300 align:middle line:84%
OK, so that's Baire
categories theorem.

00:50:35.300 --> 00:50:38.840 align:middle line:90%


00:50:38.840 --> 00:50:41.950 align:middle line:84%
Let's now use this to prove
some fundamental results

00:50:41.950 --> 00:50:43.220 align:middle line:90%
of functional analysis.

00:50:43.220 --> 00:50:46.570 align:middle line:84%
So the first result
we'll prove is

00:50:46.570 --> 00:50:56.430 align:middle line:84%
what's called the
uniform boundedness

00:50:56.430 --> 00:51:03.220 align:middle line:84%
theorem, which says that if
you have a sequence of linear--

00:51:03.220 --> 00:51:07.460 align:middle line:84%
bounded linear operators
on a Banach space,

00:51:07.460 --> 00:51:09.560 align:middle line:84%
then pointwise
boundedness implies

00:51:09.560 --> 00:51:15.910 align:middle line:84%
uniform mindedness or
uniform boundedness

00:51:15.910 --> 00:51:17.350 align:middle line:90%
in the operator norm.

00:51:17.350 --> 00:51:23.750 align:middle line:90%
So let B be a Banach space.

00:51:23.750 --> 00:51:30.080 align:middle line:90%


00:51:30.080 --> 00:51:40.500 align:middle line:84%
And let Tn be a sequence of
bounded linear operators, which

00:51:40.500 --> 00:51:47.980 align:middle line:84%
we denoted last time
by this script B,

00:51:47.980 --> 00:51:51.580 align:middle line:84%
to some other normed space,
V. So I didn't write that,

00:51:51.580 --> 00:51:54.805 align:middle line:90%
but V is a normed space.

00:51:54.805 --> 00:51:58.030 align:middle line:90%


00:51:58.030 --> 00:52:10.920 align:middle line:84%
If for all b in capital B, sup
n of Tn v-norm is less than

00:52:10.920 --> 00:52:16.500 align:middle line:84%
infinity-- so if I assume that
this sequence is pointwise

00:52:16.500 --> 00:52:18.350 align:middle line:90%
bounded--

00:52:18.350 --> 00:52:24.980 align:middle line:84%
pointwise bounded,
then I can conclude

00:52:24.980 --> 00:52:27.170 align:middle line:84%
that they're uniformly
bounded, namely

00:52:27.170 --> 00:52:33.675 align:middle line:84%
the sup of the operator
norms are bounded.

00:52:33.675 --> 00:52:43.750 align:middle line:90%


00:52:43.750 --> 00:52:45.800 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to use the
Baire category theorem.

00:52:45.800 --> 00:52:49.330 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to
write the Banach space

00:52:49.330 --> 00:52:52.690 align:middle line:90%
B as a union of closed subsets.

00:52:52.690 --> 00:52:54.700 align:middle line:84%
What will be these
closed subsets?

00:52:54.700 --> 00:53:00.370 align:middle line:84%
Remember, we're trying to find
a uniform bound on the Tn.

00:53:00.370 --> 00:53:09.460 align:middle line:84%
So just playing around, so let's
define the subset of B to Ck.

00:53:09.460 --> 00:53:17.810 align:middle line:84%
This is a set of all elements
b in B such that the norm of B

00:53:17.810 --> 00:53:27.320 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal
to 1 and sup n Tn of B

00:53:27.320 --> 00:53:28.595 align:middle line:90%
is less than or equal to k.

00:53:28.595 --> 00:53:37.830 align:middle line:90%


00:53:37.830 --> 00:53:42.495 align:middle line:84%
All right, so first
off, these are closed.

00:53:42.495 --> 00:53:47.790 align:middle line:90%


00:53:47.790 --> 00:53:51.780 align:middle line:84%
So I should say, what is k?
k here is a natural number.

00:53:51.780 --> 00:53:52.860 align:middle line:90%
Ck is closed.

00:53:52.860 --> 00:53:53.620 align:middle line:90%
Why is this?

00:53:53.620 --> 00:53:56.640 align:middle line:84%
Well, we need to show that
a sequence converging--

00:53:56.640 --> 00:53:58.950 align:middle line:84%
a sequence of elements
from Ck that converges

00:53:58.950 --> 00:54:00.990 align:middle line:90%
converges to an element of Ck.

00:54:00.990 --> 00:54:09.330 align:middle line:84%
So if Bn-- this is a
sequence of elements in Ck--

00:54:09.330 --> 00:54:18.710 align:middle line:84%
and Bn converges to B, then the
norm of b is equal to the limit

00:54:18.710 --> 00:54:23.840 align:middle line:84%
as n goes to infinity
of the norm of bn.

00:54:23.840 --> 00:54:26.930 align:middle line:84%
And each of these is
less than or equal to 1.

00:54:26.930 --> 00:54:29.900 align:middle line:84%
So the limit has to be
less than or equal to 1.

00:54:29.900 --> 00:54:36.140 align:middle line:90%


00:54:36.140 --> 00:54:41.380 align:middle line:90%
And so I shouldn't--

00:54:41.380 --> 00:54:43.810 align:middle line:90%
maybe I'll use AI a--

00:54:43.810 --> 00:54:45.000 align:middle line:90%
I have the Tn's.

00:54:45.000 --> 00:54:45.900 align:middle line:90%
I have the Bn's.

00:54:45.900 --> 00:54:52.980 align:middle line:84%
So let me use M. And for
all M in natural number,

00:54:52.980 --> 00:54:58.890 align:middle line:84%
Tm applied to be and normed,
this is equal to the limit

00:54:58.890 --> 00:55:05.310 align:middle line:84%
as m goes to infinity
of Tm Bn in norm

00:55:05.310 --> 00:55:07.920 align:middle line:84%
because these are
bounded linear operators

00:55:07.920 --> 00:55:10.910 align:middle line:90%
and therefore continuous.

00:55:10.910 --> 00:55:24.510 align:middle line:90%


00:55:24.510 --> 00:55:29.770 align:middle line:84%
OK, so Tm of B is
equal to the limit

00:55:29.770 --> 00:55:32.140 align:middle line:84%
as n goes to
infinity of Tm of Bn.

00:55:32.140 --> 00:55:34.870 align:middle line:90%


00:55:34.870 --> 00:55:41.130 align:middle line:84%
And now, the norm of
Tm of Bn is always--

00:55:41.130 --> 00:55:43.950 align:middle line:84%
well, this is always
less than or equal to k

00:55:43.950 --> 00:55:48.975 align:middle line:84%
because the Bn's are in Ck
and sup of all these guys

00:55:48.975 --> 00:55:50.100 align:middle line:90%
is less than or equal to k.

00:55:50.100 --> 00:55:54.495 align:middle line:84%
So that's also less
than or equal to k.

00:55:54.495 --> 00:55:59.290 align:middle line:90%


00:55:59.290 --> 00:56:07.840 align:middle line:84%
Thus, B is in Ck, which
implies Ck is closed.

00:56:07.840 --> 00:56:12.520 align:middle line:90%


00:56:12.520 --> 00:56:15.550 align:middle line:90%
Now, the closed ball--

00:56:15.550 --> 00:56:18.340 align:middle line:84%
or I shouldn't say-- it's not
the closure of the open ball,

00:56:18.340 --> 00:56:21.790 align:middle line:84%
but the closed ball,
b in capital B, such

00:56:21.790 --> 00:56:25.420 align:middle line:84%
that b is less
than or equal to 1.

00:56:25.420 --> 00:56:31.690 align:middle line:84%
This is equal to the
union over all k of Ck.

00:56:31.690 --> 00:56:33.370 align:middle line:90%
Why?

00:56:33.370 --> 00:56:36.670 align:middle line:84%
So first off, each of these is
contained in this closed ball.

00:56:36.670 --> 00:56:41.470 align:middle line:84%
But I'm assuming by
star that no matter what

00:56:41.470 --> 00:56:46.090 align:middle line:84%
b is in the Banach space,
sup over n of these

00:56:46.090 --> 00:56:47.450 align:middle line:90%
is less than infinity.

00:56:47.450 --> 00:56:51.760 align:middle line:84%
So there's always some
integer k given a b--

00:56:51.760 --> 00:56:54.640 align:middle line:84%
so given a certain
b in this set,

00:56:54.640 --> 00:56:58.780 align:middle line:84%
I can always find a large
enough integer such that this

00:56:58.780 --> 00:57:01.820 align:middle line:90%
is less than that integer.

00:57:01.820 --> 00:57:03.490 align:middle line:90%
So it has to--

00:57:03.490 --> 00:57:06.760 align:middle line:84%
every element in here has to
lie in one of these sets C sub k

00:57:06.760 --> 00:57:08.695 align:middle line:90%
So this is equal to the union.

00:57:08.695 --> 00:57:11.640 align:middle line:90%


00:57:11.640 --> 00:57:17.390 align:middle line:84%
So this is a
complete metric space

00:57:17.390 --> 00:57:20.090 align:middle line:84%
because it's a
closed subset of M.

00:57:20.090 --> 00:57:31.360 align:middle line:84%
So this is a complete
metric space written

00:57:31.360 --> 00:57:35.086 align:middle line:90%
as the union of a--

00:57:35.086 --> 00:57:37.150 align:middle line:90%
a union of closed subsets.

00:57:37.150 --> 00:57:50.780 align:middle line:84%
So by Baire's
theorem, there exists

00:57:50.780 --> 00:58:10.500 align:middle line:84%
one of these sets containing
an open ball of the form B, B0,

00:58:10.500 --> 00:58:11.190 align:middle line:90%
delta 0.

00:58:11.190 --> 00:58:14.870 align:middle line:90%


00:58:14.870 --> 00:58:18.577 align:middle line:84%
So one of these Ck's
contains an open ball.

00:58:18.577 --> 00:58:19.660 align:middle line:90%
Now, we're going to show--

00:58:19.660 --> 00:58:25.450 align:middle line:84%
we're going to use this k
to derive a uniform bound.

00:58:25.450 --> 00:58:32.155 align:middle line:90%
So if b is in B and--

00:58:32.155 --> 00:58:32.655 align:middle line:90%
let's see.

00:58:32.655 --> 00:58:39.680 align:middle line:90%


00:58:39.680 --> 00:58:41.060 align:middle line:90%
So let's write it this way.

00:58:41.060 --> 00:58:45.450 align:middle line:90%


00:58:45.450 --> 00:58:53.720 align:middle line:84%
If b is in the open ball of
radius delta 0 centered at 0,

00:58:53.720 --> 00:58:55.820 align:middle line:90%
so this means i.e.

00:58:55.820 --> 00:58:59.270 align:middle line:84%
the norm of b is
less than delta 0.

00:58:59.270 --> 00:59:02.370 align:middle line:90%


00:59:02.370 --> 00:59:11.870 align:middle line:84%
Then b0 plus b is in this
open ball centered at B0.

00:59:11.870 --> 00:59:14.500 align:middle line:90%


00:59:14.500 --> 00:59:29.650 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, sup n of Tn of
b0 plus b norm is less than

00:59:29.650 --> 00:59:31.540 align:middle line:90%
or equal to k.

00:59:31.540 --> 00:59:34.660 align:middle line:90%
So this ball is contained in Ck.

00:59:34.660 --> 00:59:37.060 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, if I
take the sup over n

00:59:37.060 --> 00:59:38.840 align:middle line:84%
of T sub n applied
to this thing,

00:59:38.840 --> 00:59:40.300 align:middle line:90%
this is less than or equal to k.

00:59:40.300 --> 00:59:49.560 align:middle line:90%


00:59:49.560 --> 00:59:54.720 align:middle line:84%
But then I conclude
that sup over n of Tn

00:59:54.720 --> 00:59:59.890 align:middle line:84%
applied to B, which is less
than or equal to sup n--

00:59:59.890 --> 01:00:02.200 align:middle line:84%
so I'm going to add
and subtract B0.

01:00:02.200 --> 01:00:06.850 align:middle line:90%


01:00:06.850 --> 01:00:19.040 align:middle line:84%
So let me actually write this
as sup of Tn B0 plus Tn B0

01:00:19.040 --> 01:00:24.080 align:middle line:84%
plus B. This is less
than or equal to

01:00:24.080 --> 01:00:26.420 align:middle line:90%
by the triangle inequality.

01:00:26.420 --> 01:00:28.310 align:middle line:84%
And then carrying
the sup through,

01:00:28.310 --> 01:00:40.880 align:middle line:84%
this is less than or equal to
sup n Tn B0 plus sup n Tn B0

01:00:40.880 --> 01:00:45.160 align:middle line:90%
plus B.

01:00:45.160 --> 01:00:51.610 align:middle line:84%
And so B0 is certainly in this
ball, which is contained in Ck.

01:00:51.610 --> 01:00:59.210 align:middle line:84%
So this is less
than or equal to k.

01:00:59.210 --> 01:01:04.070 align:middle line:84%
And this is still in
that ball of radius

01:01:04.070 --> 01:01:05.990 align:middle line:90%
delta 0 centered at B0.

01:01:05.990 --> 01:01:08.180 align:middle line:90%
So it lies in CK.

01:01:08.180 --> 01:01:10.100 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, this
sup is also less than

01:01:10.100 --> 01:01:11.960 align:middle line:90%
or equal to k equals 2k.

01:01:11.960 --> 01:01:17.450 align:middle line:84%
So I've shown that if I take
any element in the open ball

01:01:17.450 --> 01:01:28.150 align:middle line:84%
of radius delta 0, then the sup
over n of Tn of B is less than

01:01:28.150 --> 01:01:33.070 align:middle line:90%
or equal to 2k, all right?

01:01:33.070 --> 01:01:35.170 align:middle line:84%
And now, it's just a
simple rescaling argument

01:01:35.170 --> 01:01:38.600 align:middle line:84%
to show that the sup of
the norms are now bounded.

01:01:38.600 --> 01:01:45.970 align:middle line:90%
So suppose norm of B equals 1.

01:01:45.970 --> 01:01:59.350 align:middle line:84%
Then the norm of Tn of
delta 0 over 2 applied to B

01:01:59.350 --> 01:02:02.610 align:middle line:90%
is less than or equal to--

01:02:02.610 --> 01:02:07.320 align:middle line:84%
so for all n certainly less
than or equal to the sup over n

01:02:07.320 --> 01:02:10.110 align:middle line:84%
of all of these, which is
less than or equal to 2k

01:02:10.110 --> 01:02:13.110 align:middle line:84%
because this is an
element with norm delta

01:02:13.110 --> 01:02:15.270 align:middle line:84%
0 over 2, which is
less than delta 0.

01:02:15.270 --> 01:02:19.440 align:middle line:84%
So this is less than or
equal to 2k, which implies--

01:02:19.440 --> 01:02:21.120 align:middle line:90%
so, again Tn is linear.

01:02:21.120 --> 01:02:31.820 align:middle line:90%
So I should have reversed this.

01:02:31.820 --> 01:02:35.512 align:middle line:90%


01:02:35.512 --> 01:02:37.130 align:middle line:90%
Let M be a natural number.

01:02:37.130 --> 01:02:40.480 align:middle line:90%


01:02:40.480 --> 01:02:44.230 align:middle line:90%
Let B equal to 1.

01:02:44.230 --> 01:02:46.380 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, OK.

01:02:46.380 --> 01:02:48.810 align:middle line:84%
So then Tn applied
to B is less than

01:02:48.810 --> 01:02:52.920 align:middle line:90%
or equal to 4k over delta 0.

01:02:52.920 --> 01:02:58.950 align:middle line:84%
Now, this holds for all
B with length equal to 1.

01:02:58.950 --> 01:03:00.880 align:middle line:84%
And therefore,
the operator norm,

01:03:00.880 --> 01:03:04.230 align:middle line:84%
which is the sup over all B,
would normally equal to 1,

01:03:04.230 --> 01:03:08.430 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal
to 4k over delta 0.

01:03:08.430 --> 01:03:10.080 align:middle line:90%
This holds for all n.

01:03:10.080 --> 01:03:15.120 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, the sup
over n of the operator norm

01:03:15.120 --> 01:03:19.740 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal
to 4k over delta 0,

01:03:19.740 --> 01:03:23.115 align:middle line:84%
and therefore giving
us the uniform bound.

01:03:23.115 --> 01:03:37.360 align:middle line:90%


01:03:37.360 --> 01:03:38.320 align:middle line:90%
I have time for this?

01:03:38.320 --> 01:03:46.030 align:middle line:90%


01:03:46.030 --> 01:03:50.650 align:middle line:84%
OK, I don't think I have time
for the entire proof of what's

01:03:50.650 --> 01:03:52.530 align:middle line:90%
to come.

01:03:52.530 --> 01:03:55.720 align:middle line:84%
So I think we'll just
stop there for now.

01:03:55.720 --> 01:04:02.000 align:middle line:90%